Hi all,

I am trying to figure out how to use ezANOVA for repeated measures for
the first time and what ever I do I get this message...

Error in ezANOVA_main(data, dv, wid, within, between, observed,
diff,  :
  One or more cells is missing data.

My data is organised as....
      ttmt   pop   time    mutants
      asex   1        0     0.00000000
      asex   1        5     0.14423953
      asex   1       10     0.15668730
      asex   1       15     0.14744429
      asex   1       20     0.00000000
      asex   1       22     0.13912341
      asex   2        0     0.00000000
      asex   2        5     0.00000000
etc.....

with 3 treatments, 8 populations per treatment, each measured at 6
time points.

the model I've been trying to run is....
m1 <- ezANOVA(
	data = df.1
	, dv = .(mutants)
	, wid = .(pop)
	, within = .(time)
	, between = .(ttmt)
)

My table is balanced, with no empty cells.  I'm so confused, any help
would be greatly appreciated!

many thanks

Ellie

From ucb25d91fd630a4e5 at groups.google.com  Fri Nov 25 15:47:16 2011
From: ucb25d91fd630a4e5 at groups.google.com (Mike Lawrence)
Date: Fri, 25 Nov 2011 15:47:16 +0000
Subject: Re: [ez4r] Error in ezANOVA_main......... One or more cells is missing data.
Message-ID: <-2hjt8eiULc.9CxDgi7-W4kJ@ez4r.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <-2hjt8eiULc.kl9SOoIIIVoJ@ez4r.groups.google.com>
References: <-2hjt8eiULc.kl9SOoIIIVoJ@ez4r.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ez4r/c/-2hjt8eiULc/m/9CxDgi7-W4kJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Use ezDesign() to check the balance of your data:

ezDesign(
    data = df.1
    , x = .(time)
    , y = .(pop)
    , col = .(ttmt)
)

missing data will come out as uncolored (grey) patches.

By the way, it looks like you're analyzing proportions. If you have
the raw 1s and 0s from which those proportions are derived, it would
be much better to use mixed effects modelling with pop as a random
effect, ttmt and time as fixed effects, and a binomial link function
for error. See the arguments by Dixon (2008;
http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.jml.2007.11.004) and Jaeger (2008;
http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.jml.2007.11.007) for why this is
necessary. Also, you should consider using a tool that can capture
potential non-linearities in the effect of time, such as generalized
additive mixed effects modelling (GAMM). Happily, the dev version of
ez (https://github.com/mike-lawrence/ez) automatically incorporates
GAMM for continuous fixed effects in the ezMixed function.

